course: dictionary
lesson: 1063
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. «Bottom» is a good example. Read the words around it to find out which meaning the writer wants.
explain: «A swing is fastest at the bottom and slowest at the top.» Here bottom means the lowest point. Try your meaning in the sentence: "the lowest point is fastest" makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: «A punt is a flat-bottomed boat pushed along with a long pole on shallow rivers.» Flat-bottomed means the flat base of the boat, the part at the bottom. Context clues help you work out the meaning even when the word is new.
fact: The bottom is the lowest part of something.
fact: The bottom of a lake or the sea is the ground under the water.
fact: Your bottom is the part of your body you sit on.
fact: «At the bottom» of a list or a class means the last place.
fact: «Get to the bottom of» something means to find the real cause, as in «get to the bottom of the mystery».
exercise: «The bottom shelf is full of picture books.» What does bottom mean here? | The ground under the shelf | The last page of a book | *The lowest shelf | The top shelf
exercise: «The fish swam slowly along the bottom of the lake.» What is the bottom here? | The end of the swim | The top of the lake | The part of the fish's body | *The ground under the water
exercise: Mia says Jordan is at the bottom of the race. What does she mean? | *He finished last | He is sitting on the ground | He won the race | He fell into the lake
test: «Bubbles start at tiny specks in the bottom of a glass.» What does bottom mean here? | The handle of the glass | The rim at the top of the glass | The water inside the glass | *The lowest part of the glass
test: «The detective wants to get to the bottom of the theft.» What does get to the bottom of mean? | Dig a hole in the ground | Sit down on the floor | *Find the real cause | Finish the drink
exam: Which sentence uses bottom to mean the part of your body you sit on? | «She is at the bottom of the class.» | «The bottom of the ladder was muddy.» | «The bottom of the lake is rocky.» | *«He landed with a bump on his bottom.»
